-- Passage rg9-authors-purpose-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3a1704a8-3b51-5fe3-b2c2-e198b767e287', 'rg9-authors-purpose-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Footbridge That Failed in Public$q$, $q$From the introduction to a short history of public works, written by Rafael Quintero, chief engineer for the city of Westmere, for the city's website.

When the Calder Street footbridge opened in 1974, the city celebrated it as the most modern structure in Westmere. Its deck was made of smooth steel plates painted a bright blue, and photographs from opening day show crowds crossing it in their best clothes. Within a month, the complaints began. On rainy mornings, the painted steel became as slick as ice, and several walkers slipped and fell.

The first response from the engineering office was not one we are proud of. A city statement suggested that pedestrians were wearing unsuitable shoes and should walk more carefully. For nearly a year, the bridge stayed exactly as it was, and residents began walking an extra half mile to cross the river on the old road bridge instead.

What changed things was a young engineer named Ingrid Solberg, who spent several rainy weeks standing on the bridge with a notebook. She recorded every slip, measured how water pooled on the plates, and tested sample panels with different surfaces. Her report was blunt: the problem was the design, not the people using it. The city added a gritty coating to the deck, and the falls stopped almost immediately.

I tell this story to every new engineer we hire, and I tell it here for the same reason. It would be easy to leave it out of our official history, which is mostly a list of successes. But the most useful thing the Calder Street footbridge ever did was fail in public. Because of it, every walking surface the city builds is now tested in wet conditions before it opens.

A city that cannot admit its mistakes is a city that repeats them. The blue paint is long gone from that bridge, but the lesson it taught is still holding us up.$q$, 'draft')
