-- Passage rg9-authors-purpose-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('35cd9fa5-dae1-53c2-8ac2-408db2fc21f0', 'rg9-authors-purpose-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$In Defense of the Crooked Carrot$q$, $q$From an essay by Thandiwe Mokoena, who grows vegetables on a small farm outside Ridgeford, written for a regional magazine read mostly by shoppers.

Last fall, I pulled a carrot out of the ground that had split into two legs and twisted around itself like a dancer. My nephew laughed and named it Gerald. Then he asked what we would do with it, and I had to admit the truth: a carrot like Gerald almost never reaches a grocery store shelf.

Most shoppers never see the vegetables that farmers leave behind. Stores often set strict rules about size, shape, and color, because they believe customers will only buy produce that looks perfect. A cucumber that curves too much or an apple with a small brown freckle may be turned away, even though it tastes exactly the same. Some of this produce goes to animal feed, but a great deal of it is simply plowed back into the field.

I do not blame the stores entirely. They are responding to what they think we want. When was the last time you reached past a shiny tomato to choose the lumpy one beside it? Most of us, myself included, have been trained to judge food with our eyes instead of our tongues.

That habit can change. Some markets in our region have started selling oddly shaped produce at a lower price, and many of them report that it sells out quickly. Every crooked carrot that ends up in a soup pot is one less carrot whose water, fuel, and hours of labor were wasted.

So the next time you shop, look for the strange ones. Ask your store manager whether they carry them. Buy the pepper with the dent and the potato with the odd bump. It will taste just as good, and you will be telling the people who sell food that you care more about flavor than appearance.

As for Gerald, he went into a stew. He was delicious.$q$, 'draft')
