   $q$How does the ending, "As for Gerald, he went into a stew. He was delicious," support the author's argument?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It shifts the essay from an argument about produce to a recipe that readers can try at home.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-purpose"},{"text":"It admits that the argument is mostly a joke and should not be taken seriously by shoppers.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"It suggests that the author no longer wants to sell oddly shaped carrots to grocery stores.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It returns to the opening story and offers a short, humorous proof that an odd shape does not hurt flavor.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It returns to the opening story and offers a short, humorous proof that an odd shape does not hurt flavor.$q$, $q$Calling Gerald "delicious" echoes the author's claim that odd produce "will taste just as good," and it ties the ending back to the opening. No recipe is given, so the essay does not become one.$q$, 'draft', '35cd9fa5-dae1-53c2-8ac2-408db2fc21f0')
