-- Passage rg9-authors-purpose-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ed8d4aa5-ea67-5d94-b46e-4997ce84cee9', 'rg9-authors-purpose-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Pass Before the Pass$q$, $q$From a sports column by Marisol Ferrante, who has covered high school soccer for the Lakeshore Gazette for fifteen years.

Ask any fan who won last Saturday's regional final, and they will tell you about Devon Castellanos. His curling shot in the seventy-eighth minute found the top corner, and the crowd poured onto the field. The photo of his celebration made our front page. It deserved to.

But I want to tell you about a different moment, one that lasted about two seconds and that almost nobody cheered. Thirty seconds before the goal, a defender named Priya Raman won the ball near her own penalty area. Instead of booting it away, as most players would have under that kind of pressure, she looked up and threaded a pass between two opponents to a midfielder at the center line. That pass turned a dangerous moment into an attack. Without it, there is no goal.

Soccer, like most sports, keeps score in a way that rewards the final touch. Goals get counted, and so do the passes directly before them, which are called assists. Everything earlier disappears from the record. A player like Raman, whose best work happens far from the net, can play a brilliant season and finish it with almost nothing next to her name in the statistics.

Some coaches already track what they call the pass before the pass, and a few leagues have begun publishing it. I think every league should. Statistics shape what young players believe matters. If the numbers only celebrate scorers, then every eleven-year-old on a youth team will want to be a striker, and fewer will learn the patient, unglamorous skills that win games.

Castellanos earned his headline. I only wish the scoreboard had room for one more name.$q$, 'draft')
