-- Passage rg9-authors-purpose-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f24dc6a5-9f39-514d-9926-0117136c9ec1', 'rg9-authors-purpose-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Let Them Draw$q$, $q$From an open letter by Rosalind Ngata, an art teacher at Carrow High School, published in the Carrow Weekly.

To the trustees of the Carrow Museum of Art:

Last month, I brought twenty-two ninth graders to your museum. Many of them had never been inside an art museum before, and I watched them walk into the main gallery with a mix of nervousness and wonder. Then a guard asked them to put away their sketchbooks.

I understand the reason for the rule. Your staff explained that a few years ago, a visitor's pen leaked onto a gallery floor, and that crowds of sketchers can block the paintings. Those are fair concerns, and I do not dismiss them. A museum must protect its collection and keep its rooms open to everyone.

But please consider what my students lost. When you sketch a painting, you have to look at it far longer than you would otherwise. You notice how the light falls on a sleeve, where the artist put the darkest shadow, how a face is built from a few quick strokes. Drawing is not a distraction from looking. For many young people, it is the best way to look closely.

Other museums have found simple solutions. Some allow pencils only, with no pens or paint. Some set aside certain hours or certain rooms for sketching, or hand out small clipboards at the entrance. None of these changes would cost much, and all of them would protect your floors.

My students left your museum having seen beautiful things. I want the next group to leave having truly studied them. I would be glad to help write a sketching policy that works for your staff and for young artists, and I hope you will accept that offer.$q$, 'draft')
